#definition-box(title: "Transhumance")[
  *Transhumance* is the seasonal, cyclical migration of pastoralists and their herds between two distinct ecological zones:
  - *Vertical Transhumance:* Moving from low-altitude valleys to high-altitude mountain pastures in *summer*, and returning to valley bottoms in *winter*. Prominent Indian Himalayan pastoral tribes include the *Gaddis*, *Bakarwals*, *Gujjars*, and *Bhotiyas*.
  - *Latitudinal Transhumance:* Moving from the frozen tundra southward into the sub-Arctic taiga forest during extreme winter months, as practiced by reindeer herders in Siberia.
]

=== 2. Commercial Livestock Rearing
In contrast to nomadic herding, commercial livestock rearing is organized on scientific, capital-intensive, and market-oriented foundations.
- *Permanent Ranches:* Animals are reared on vast, permanently fenced enclosures called *ranches*. Ranches are subdivided into parcels; herds are systematically rotated from one paddock to another to prevent overgrazing.
- *Mono-Animal Specialization:* Only one species of animal (predominantly cattle, sheep, goats, or horses) is reared on a single ranch.
- *Scientific Management:* Rearing practices emphasize selective breeding, genetic improvement, disease control, veterinary care, and mechanized shearing/slaughtering.
- *Major World Regions:* The temperate grasslands of the *United States*, *Argentina*, *Uruguay*, *Australia*, and *New Zealand*.

