= Module 2: Global Agricultural Systems & Mining

== 2.1 Typology of World Agricultural Systems
Agriculture varies from primitive self-sufficiency farming to highly sophisticated industrial agribusinesses.

#figure(
  image("assets/geog_act_agriculture_typologies.png", width: 82%),
  caption: [Comparative Framework of Global Agricultural Typologies and Farming Systems],
)

=== 1. Primitive Subsistence Agriculture (Shifting Cultivation)
- *Technique:* Also termed *Slash-and-Burn* or *Bush-Fallowing*. Vegetation is cleared by felling and fire; ashes add potash to the nutrient-deficient tropical soil. After 3 to 5 years, soil fertility is exhausted, weeds invade, and farmers abandon the plot to clear a fresh patch of forest.
- *Tools:* Rudimentary implements (digging sticks, hoes, machetes); no animal traction, chemical fertilizers, or modern machinery.
- *Regional Nomenclature (High-Yield CUET Matching):*
  - *Jhuming:* North-Eastern States of India (Assam, Meghalaya, Nagaland, Mizoram).
  - *Milpa:* Central America and Mexico.
  - *Ladang:* Indonesia and Malaysia.
  - *Roca:* Brazil.
  - *Taungya:* Myanmar.
  - *Chenging:* Philippines.
  - *Caingin / Bewar / Dahiya:* Pockets of Central India.

=== 2. Intensive Subsistence Agriculture
Practiced in densely populated regions of Monsoon Asia. It is divided into two distinct subtypes:
- *Dominated by Wet Paddy:* Characterized by rice dominance. Manual labor is pervasive; landholdings are tiny and fragmented; draft cattle provide traction; farmyard manure is heavily applied. *Yield per unit of land is exceptionally high, but yield per agricultural laborer is relatively low.*
- *Dominated by Crops Other Than Paddy:* Practiced where rainfall is insufficient or relief is rugged (Northern China, Manchuria, North-West India). Primary crops include *wheat*, *soybean*, *barley*, and *millets/sorghum*.

=== 3. Plantation Agriculture
Introduced by European colonial powers in tropical and subtropical colonies to supply raw materials and beverages to metropolitan markets.
- *Distinctive Characteristics:* Huge land estates (*latifundia*), massive capital investment, managerial and technical expertise, cheap indigenous labor, and single-crop specialization.
- *Colonial Legacies:*
  - *French:* Established cocoa and coffee plantations in West Africa (Ivory Coast, Ghana).
  - *British:* Developed tea estates in India (Assam, Darjeeling) and Sri Lanka; rubber plantations in Malaysia; sugarcane and banana estates in the West Indies.
  - *Dutch:* Monopolized sugarcane plantations in Indonesia (Java).
  - *Spanish & Americans:* Invested heavily in coconut and sugarcane plantations in the Philippines.
  - *Portuguese:* Established vast coffee estates called *Fazendas* in Brazil, which remain prominent today.

=== 4. Extensive Commercial Grain Cultivation
- *Biome:* Practiced in the interior continental sub-humid temperate grasslands (*Steppes* of Eurasia, *Prairies* of North America, *Pampas* of Argentina, *Velds* of South Africa, *Downs* of Australia, and *Canterbury Plains* of New Zealand).
- *Crop Dominance:* *Wheat* is the undisputed principal crop; secondary crops include corn, barley, oats, and rye.
- *Mechanization & Yield Paradox:* Fully mechanized farming from plowing to combine harvesting on massive farms (often exceeding 1,000 hectares).
  #formula-box(title: "The Extensive Grain Productivity Paradox")[
    $ "Yield per Unit Area (Hectare)" = bold("Low") wide <==> wide "Yield per Laborer / Worker" = bold("Exceptionally High") $
    Because the human labor force is minuscule relative to the colossal land surface, output per farm laborer is among the highest on earth, even though per-hectare productivity is lower than intensive Asian wet-paddy farming.
  ]

=== 5. Mixed Farming & Dairy Farming
- *Mixed Farming:* Equal emphasis on crop cultivation and livestock rearing. Crops cultivated include wheat, barley, oats, rye, maize, and crucial fodder crops (alfalfa, clover). *Crop rotation* and *leguminous nitrogen-fixing crops* preserve soil vitality. Found in North-Western Europe, Eastern North America, parts of Eurasia, and temperate South America.
- *Dairy Farming:* The most advanced, efficient, and capital-intensive form of livestock farming:
  - *Labor Demand:* Extremely labor-intensive year-round; cattle require systematic feeding, milch hygiene, and disease monitoring without any seasonal slack.
  - *Infrastructure:* High capital expenditure on animal sheds, automated milking machines, silo storage, pasteurizers, and refrigerated transport.
  - *Spatial Distribution:* Concentrated strictly near massive urban-industrial agglomerations which provide immediate consumer markets for liquid milk and perishable dairy products.
  - *Three Principal Belts:*
    1. *North-Western Europe:* (Largest belt stretching across Britain, Denmark, Netherlands, Sweden, Germany).
    2. *Canada & Northern USA:* (Stretching across the Great Lakes to the Atlantic seaboard).
    3. *South-Eastern Australia, New Zealand, and Tasmania.*

=== 6. Mediterranean Agriculture & Viticulture
- *Climatic Setting:* Confined to regions bordering the Mediterranean Sea in Southern Europe and North Africa, Central California, Central Chile, the south-western tip of South Africa (Cape Town), and South-Western Australia.
- *Specialization:* Highly commercial farming characterized by citrus fruits (*oranges, lemons, grapefruits*), olives, and figs.
- *Viticulture (Grape Cultivation):* The signature hallmark of Mediterranean agriculture. Superior quality grapes are fermented into world-renowned vintage wines (e.g., Bordeaux, Champagne, Chianti), while inferior or dried grades are dehydrated into raisins, sultanas, and currants.

=== 7. Market Gardening, Truck Farming & Collective Systems
- *Market Gardening & Horticulture:* Cultivation of high-value crops (vegetables, fruits, and flowers) exclusively for urban consumers on small farms located near urban centers.
  #tip-box(title: "Truck Farming Explained")[
    The specialized cultivation of vegetables where the distance of farms from the urban market is governed by the distance a truck can traverse overnight is universally termed *Truck Farming*. The term originates from the Middle English word "truck", meaning barter or conveyance of commodities.
  ]
- *Factory Farming:* Industrial livestock rearing in advanced Western economies where poultry, pigs, and cattle are stall-fed in climate-controlled indoor enclosures on manufactured feed, monitored by computerized health systems.
- *Co-operative vs. Collective Farming:*
  - *Co-operative Farming:* Farmers voluntarily pool their resources (machinery, purchase of inputs, marketing of produce) while retaining private land ownership. Exceptionally successful in Northern Europe, most notably *Denmark* (where virtually every farmer is a co-operative member), Netherlands, Belgium, and Sweden.
  - *Collective Farming (Kolkhoz):* Introduced in the former USSR under Soviet collectivization. Farmers surrendered private land, livestock, and machinery to collective state-managed farms, retaining only small private garden plots for personal subsistence.

== 2.2 Mining: Physical Factors and Exploitation Methods
Mining involves the extraction of valuable mineral deposits from the earth's lithosphere.

*Determinants of Mining Activity:*
1. *Physical Factors:* Size, depth, grade, and geological structure of the mineral ore body.
2. *Economic Factors:* Global demand for the mineral, availability of capital, extraction technology, transportation infrastructure, and labor supply.

